-- checker's memory on a 6.4 GB machine.
-- Determinacy as the checker uses it (derived 2026-11-15): conversion needs only soundness, so it is
-- written as a decision over two NN derivations, Dec1 of a path between their meanings, refusing
-- where the constructors differ (a catch-all clause, no refutations: the host cannot unify through
-- NN's tupled index, tested) or a component's decision refuses. Its hypotheses are paths between the
-- two indices' contexts and shapes, and its result a path of meanings over them (PathP over the
-- context path; dependent composition by comp with the two ends as the system). Each matched case
-- reassembles the rule's data from what the components decide: a λ is abs-mean of its domain and
-- body (pull-lift-id, fmap-id), its body's context a congruence of ext-h at the domain after
-- dmap-fmap; application's, a pair's and case's data by retype from the projections the premises
-- carry; Bool's types by contraction; a variable by its word. conv = reify both types at their
-- computability, code-dec, then this decision; the checker is elab-n at conv.
-- Host facts for this proof (tested 2026-11-15): pattern matching on two values of an indexed family
-- unifies indices that are constructor applications (two derivations at one index need only the
-- matching clauses), but not through a pair, and a data family whose later index's type depends on an
-- earlier index is refused (the constructors' indices are checked against fresh variables), so NN
-- keeps its tupled index and the decision takes each derivation at its own index with paths between
-- their contexts and shapes. Word numerals are not patterns (w-dec decides them).
-- Derivations as one family over a rule table (2026-11-16, lib/nbe-model.cub): a rule (DRule) is its data
-- (RData, one record per shape: x-app, x-proj, ...), its conclusion (concl), its premises (premJ, each a
-- role and a judgment) and the tags it concludes at (kallows on its kind). One data family D over a goal
-- (one tagged judgment, or a list) has three constructors: dnil, dcons and drule; Der is D at tag any, NN
-- at ne and nf, a premise's tag its role's at the conclusion's (rtag, split on the tag first so any
-- reduces at every role). One recursion d-fold takes an algebra, one clause per rule's meaning: embed
-- (one clause for the coercion, one for every other rule), renaming (ren-alg, at ne/nf; ren-ne) and the
-- fundamental lemma (fund-alg, at any); a disallowed rule-tag pair is Empty-rec of its kallows proof.
-- by-rule builds a derivation from its premises' tuple; the old constructor names are smart
-- constructors over it. Generic in the bit: pend and tend (path ends), fund-con, fund-pend; fund-tfam
-- serves Π and Σ. Host facts (tested): ok is reserved and cannot be a binder; an implicit index is not
-- inferred through a function (NN KJ = D (g-one (nk-tag (fst KJ)) ..), so callers give J or use ren-ne);
-- data fields may be PathP but not Path; clauses match records but not pairs; a clause with a variable
-- where the rule's record could be matched made the fundamental lemma's case tree exhaust memory.
-- Of the five places above, done (2026-11-16): the NN/Der pair (one family; d-tr, d-fm-in/out at a tag),
-- the term/type pairs (JC with jc-tr, jc-fm, comp-j over JK), and the cube layer; the skip lemmas lost
-- their unused generality (diag-skip/-d at the identity). Open: the checker's per-rule assembly as one
-- generic case, and Π/Σ at the type level (Fam, CompE's type clause, rty, dty; fund-tfam already serves both).
